26	+# ADR-0089: Bound optional Claude start-refusal diagnosis
27	+
28	+Status: Accepted under delegated delivery decision D203, 2026-11-21.
29	+
30	+## Context
31	+
32	+After a native spawn refusal with no child PID, the Claude runner knows that nothing
33	+was submitted. Read-only filesystem observations can distinguish a missing absolute
34	+executable or missing/file working directory from an unknown cause (#364). Waiting
35	+for those optional observations must not postpone the known failure indefinitely.
36	+Normal turns have no request timeout, and the inference watchdog and run deadline
37	+are optional. A filesystem metadata read can remain pending after Node delivers both
38	+the refusal and the child close.
39	+
40	+The third general audit identified this liveness defect. It did not observe a
41	+naturally stalled Windows filesystem. The earlier output-loss and help-only failure
42	+corrections remain necessary and retain their existing precedence.
43	+
44	+## Decision
45	+
46	+Give optional diagnosis a private absolute monotonic deadline 1,000 ms after the
47	+runner observes a genuine no-PID refusal. It applies independently of the caller's
48	+request timeout, including null. Timely factual observations retain the existing
49	+fixed cause. On expiry return the actual spawn code with unknown cause and definite
50	+non-submission. Expiry does not mean that a process timed out, was cancelled or had
51	+a missing executable/directory.
52	+
53	+Check the elapsed budget before each metadata lookup, when accepting its result and
54	+when the diagnostic timer runs. A delayed event-loop callback cannot make late
55	+metadata timely. Earlier request deadlines and cancellation settle promptly through
56	+the existing outcome rules. Every settlement clears the owned diagnostic timer and
57	+removes the abort listener. Late metadata cannot change the result or initiate a
58	+follow-on lookup. Node may still finish an already issued native `stat`; this bounds
59	+the wait, without claiming filesystem cancellation.
60	+
61	+Started-process output loss, uncertain delivery and stronger failures retain their
62	+existing behavior. There is no additional launch, kill of a nonexistent child,
63	+retry, fallback, PATH resolver, dependency or public configuration option.
64	+
65	+## Alternatives
66	+
67	+- Rely on the request timeout or watchdog: declined because supported defaults
68	+  provide neither bound.
69	+- Return unknown immediately for every refusal: declined because fast local
70	+  metadata provides useful factual diagnoses without further execution.
71	+- Use synchronous filesystem access or a new worker/process subsystem: declined
72	+  because either blocking the event loop or adding an execution boundary is
73	+  disproportionate to this optional diagnostic.
74	+- Add user-facing configuration or automatic retry: declined because a known failed
75	+  start needs a conservative terminal result, not another turn or policy choice.
76	+
77	+## Consequences
78	+
79	+One second allows ordinary local metadata while bounding the known refusal under
80	+default settings. Slow or unavailable metadata produces the existing generic
81	+unknown-cause failure instead of a specific diagnosis. The event loop must run to
82	+deliver any JavaScript result; the monotonic check prevents late cause acceptance
83	+when it resumes. This is neither a hard real-time guarantee nor native stat cancellation.
84	+
85	+Maintained tests exercise the public harness and real runner with successful
86	+prerequisites, a refused default turn and a controlled pending stat, plus timely and
87	+late metadata, earlier deadlines/abort, cleanup and started-process controls. An
88	+old-source control must fail the finite default-turn liveness oracle. Controlled
89	+metadata delays remain distinct from native refusal observations and a naturally
90	+occurring filesystem stall. No schema, stored-content, environment, coverage-policy
91	+or owner acceptance change is required. Independent corrective review, separate QA,
92	+current CI/native verification and coordinator completion remain delivery gates.
